WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.180 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  [Applause]  13 years ago it was my freshman year of  college  2006 and I was attending my first  meeting of the Association of black  Harvard women I was so excited to meet  fellow black women classmates for the  first time we went around in a circle  introducing ourselves the first woman to  go happen to have a Nigerian name she  said hi my name is adora East Cunanan a  it means a child who is one of the  community who was given to all and whom  almost looked after then the second girl  went she said hi my name is Saran  al-azhar it means a sacred flower one  found in the Bible that is especially  sacred then it was my turn I said hi my  name is Mercedes vent it means well it's  kind of just like the car that moment  seeded a curiosity in me about African  names  I was so interest interested in the  beauty of the names 12 years later I  would find myself leading a trip to  Ghana Nigeria with Stanford and Harvard  classmates where we were going to  explore our heritage and our roots I  call head started calling the trip my  black birthright when I think back on  what inspired me to plan such a trip I  think back to that day my freshman year  of college into two other times the  second time was my was also in college  when I first learned about the Jewish  birthright or Taglit birthright Israel  trip my friends in college told me about  this all-expenses-paid trip where they  got to go visit Israel learn about their  heritage connect to their people and get  really undocked Rene de Dinh what it  meant to be Jewish I was like that's so  cool why don't we have that  the third incident that inspired the  trip was a few years later when I was in  the working world  it was 2016 and I was sitting at my desk  one day when my phone and computer  started blowing up with notifications I  clicked on a link and what I saw next  horrified me there was a video streaming  on Facebook live of a man who had just  been shot his fiancee or a girlfriend  was sitting next to him in the side seat  was filming the aftermath her  four-year-old daughter was in the  backseat  they had just he had just been shot  after being pulled over by the police on  a routine traffic stop and had been shot  about seven times and 40 seconds my mind  started racing  I started thinking why did someone shoot  into the car so quickly right after a  routine traffic stop  why would someone shoot into the car was  there there was a little girl in the  backseat  what if it had been my dad or brother  and the front seat what if I had been  the little girl in the back his name was  Phil and Oh Castel that was an incident  that jolted me very deeply the rest of  the day at work I tried to focus on  getting things done and being productive  - member of the team but unfortunately  my teammates didn't get anything from me  the rest of the day I couldn't focus  later that week I called my friend and I  said we've got to do something we've got  to help black people we looked into how  we could support economic empowerment  initiatives or how we could support  black lives matter we donated in rally  but nothing quite stuck we were still  yearning to do more it would take  another two years before I found a  solution that did stick and it ended up  being my trip to Ghana and Nigeria I  didn't realize it at the time that it  would become a solution but it did end  up being one in a way the trip itself  was full of new and exciting adventures  we went to concerts where we danced all  night - afro beats I learned how to cook  jollof rice a dish that is a staple of  African diets and has inspired intense  rivalry I got my hair braided we  discussed Nigerian politics and history  and I even got a new name so I  participated in a traditional Nigerian  naming ceremony where they gave me a  name as Sosa Ottawa ole okay as Sosa  means God's gift  Ottawa means born on a Monday and all  okay is the family name they said to me  you will always have a family here in  Nigeria welcome to the family for the  first time in my life I felt a surge of  gratitude and appreciation as I realized  I finally felt connected to the people  of the African continent to my people  over the course of the trip I realized  that my mind was really being put at  ease that the recurring racial  soundtracked caused by microaggressions  in the United States was starting to  lessen that I was healing mentally that  I was healing through heritage over the  course of the trip I also thought about  well you know what if I lived here in  Ghana or in Nigeria I wouldn't be  subjected to as many racially charged  microaggressions and I wouldn't have to  wonder if I was hired for my token or  for my potential research shows that the  development of the ethnic and racial  identity can have really positive  benefits for minorities youth and young  adults these positive benefits can  include higher self esteem greater  confidence and even higher academic  achievement in school research has also  shown that in interventions rooted in  indigenous traditions have had the  I had had the effect of decreasing  substance abuse and attempts at suicide  so what I'm talking about here the  developing and ethnic and racial  identity and connecting to your heritage  is not just a matter of having higher  self esteem or having greater confidence  although those could be great benefits  it could be a matter of life or death I  strongly believe that anybody any  displaced person who has been displaced  or up whether due to fling for a new  country or simply emigrating for a  better life can benefit from planning  their own birth rate trip and finding  some of the healing that I did so where  do I go from here i as I said thought  about you know if I lived in the night  Ghana Nigeria some of the healing  benefits I would get but if I lived  there I want to get to live here in the  United States where we have the greatest  melting pot experiment going on that the  world's ever seen  call me an optimist but I still share  Martin Luther King's dream that one day  we won't be judged by the color of our  skin I'm committed to helping more young  adults more youth of minoritized  backgrounds go on trips that such as the  one as I did I've gotten involved with  an organization called birthright Africa  and I'm hoping that together we can help  them raise money to send more people on  these trips I hope that in sharing my  story today with you guys my short story  of the healing that I went through that  you're either inspired to go find your  own birthright or support those of us on  this journey thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

